@startuml
' Purpose: Score alias mapping into policy evaluation scope
' Source of truth:
'   src/api/service/journeyResolver.ts
'   src/api/service/utils.ts
'   src/api/service/forms.ts

title Score Aliasing - Scope Mapping Sequence Diagram

participant "Service\n(forms.ts / policy.ts)" as Service
participant "buildPolicyScopeWithAliases" as JR
participant "JourneyTemplateEntry\n(effective steps)" as Entry
participant "PolicyScope" as Scope
participant "PolicyParser" as PP

Service -> JR : buildPolicyScopeWithAliases(responses, patientId)
JR -> Scope : buildPolicyScope(responses)\nraw answers + scores\n{ OSS.total: 22, PNRS_2: 6 }
JR -> JR : pick most recent ACTIVE journey\n(none -> return base scope)
loop each effective step with scoreAliases
  JR -> Entry : read scoreAliases\n{ "OSS.total": "OSS_week4",\n  "PNRS_2": "PNRS_week4" }
  JR -> JR : latest response where\njourneyTemplateEntryId == step.id
  JR -> Scope : scope[alias] = scores[rawKey]\n(fallback: Number(answers[rawKey]))
end
Scope --> Service : Final numeric scope\n{ OSS.total: 22, PNRS_2: 6,\n  OSS_week4: 22, PNRS_week4: 6 }
Service -> PP : evaluateExpression(rule.expression, scope)

note right
  Aliasing is computed in:
  src/api/service/journeyResolver.ts
  buildPolicyScopeWithAliases(...)

  scoreAliasLabels are not added to the scope;
  they label variables in the policy editor
  and journal templates.

  Benefit:
  Policy authors can write stable,
  time-point-specific identifiers
  independent of raw scoring key names.
end note

@enduml
